== 1. Poring on Sin More than on the Savior

#align(center)[The third thing to be shown is the various devices that Satan has to keep souls in a sad, doubting, questioning, and uncomfortable condition.#footnote[The blessed Bradford, in one of his letters, says this: “O Lord, at times it seems to me that I feel it so with me, as if there were no difference between my heart and the wicked. I have a blind mind, as they do; a proud, stubborn, rebellious, hard heart, as they do,” and so he goes on, etc.]]

Though Satan can never rob a believer of his crown, yet such is his malice and envy that he will leave no stone unturned, no means untried, to rob believers of their comfort and peace, to make their life a burden and a hell to them, to cause them to spend their days in sorrow and mourning, in sighing and complaining, in doubting and questioning: surely we have no share in Christ; our graces are not true; our hopes are the hopes of hypocrites; our confidence is only presumption; our enjoyments are only delusions, etc.

I will show you this in some particulars.

#strong[The first #emph[device] that Satan has to keep souls in a sad, doubting, and questioning condition, and so to make their life a hell, is,]

BY causing them to be always poring#footnote[A Christian should wear Christ in his bosom as a flower of delight, for he is a whole paradise of delight. He who does not mind Christ more than his sin can never be as thankful and fruitful as he should be.] and musing on sin, to mind their sins more than their Savior; yes, so to mind their sins as to forget, yes, to neglect their Savior, so that, as the psalmist says, “The Lord is not in all their thoughts.” Their eyes are so fixed on their disease that they cannot see the #emph[remedy,] though it is near; and they so muse on their debts that they have neither mind nor heart to think of their surety, etc.

#strong[Now the first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is,]

For weak believers to consider that though Jesus Christ has not freed them from the presence of sin, yet he#footnote[#emph[Peccata enim non nocent, si non placent:] my sins do not hurt me, if they do not please me. Sin is like that wild fig tree, or the ivy in the wall: cut off stump, trunk, bough, and branches, and yet some root or other will sprout out again until the wall is pulled down. Rom 8:1.] has freed them from the damning power of sin. It is most true that sin and grace were never born together, and sin and grace will not die together; yet while a believer breathes in this world, they must live together; they must keep house together. Christ in this life will not free any believer from the presence of any one sin, though he does free every believer from the damning power of every sin: “There is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus, who do not walk according to the flesh but according to the Spirit.” The law cannot condemn a believer, for Christ has fulfilled it for him. Divine justice cannot condemn him, for Christ has satisfied it. His sins cannot condemn him, for they are pardoned in the blood of Christ. And his own conscience (on righteous grounds) cannot condemn him, because Christ, who is greater than his conscience, has acquitted him, etc.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that though Jesus Christ has not freed you from the molesting and vexing power of sin, yet he has freed you from the reign and#footnote[The early Christians chose rather to be thrown to lions without than left to lusts within. #emph[Ad leonem magis quam ad lenonem,] says Tertullian. Rom 6:14.] dominion of sin. You say that sin so molests and vexes you that you cannot think of God, nor go to God, nor speak with God. Oh, but remember, it is one thing for sin to molest and vex you, and another thing for sin to reign and have dominion over you. “For sin shall not have dominion over you#super[+], because you are not under law, but under grace.” Sin may rebel, but it will never reign in any saint. It goes with sin in the regenerate as with those beasts that Daniel speaks of: “their dominion was removed,#footnote[Dan 7:12.] but they were granted an extension of life for a season and a time.”

Now sin reigns in the soul when the soul willingly and readily obeys it, and submits to its commands, as subjects actively obey and embrace the commands of their prince. The commands#footnote[It is a sign that sin has not gained your#super[+] consent, but has committed a rape on your souls, when you cry out to God. If the violated virgin under the law cried out, she was guiltless (Deut 22:27). So when sin plays the tyrant over the soul, and the soul cries out, it is guiltless; those sins will not be charged to the soul.] of a king are readily embraced and obeyed by his subjects, but the commands of a tyrant are embraced and obeyed unwillingly. All the service done to a tyrant comes from violence, and not from obedience. A free and willing subjection to the commands of sin shows the soul to be under the reign and dominion of sin; but from this plague, this hell, Christ frees all believers. Sin cannot say of a believer what the centurion said of his servants: “I tell one to go, and he goes; and another to come, and he comes. I tell my servant to do something, and he does it.” No, the heart of a saint rises against the commands of sin; and when sin would carry his soul to the devil, he drags his sin before the Lord and cries out for justice. “Lord,” says the believing soul, “sin plays the tyrant, the devil, in me. It would have me do what works against your holiness as well as against my happiness, against your honor and glory as well as my comfort and peace. Therefore do me justice, you righteous Judge of heaven and earth, and let this tyrant sin die for it,” etc.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is constantly to keep one eye on the promises of the remission of sin,#footnote[Isa 44:22. Mic 7:18, 19. Col 2:13, 14. The promises of God are a precious book; every leaf drops myrrh and mercy.] as well as the other eye on the inward operations of sin. It is most certainly true that God will graciously pardon to his people the sins that he will not fully subdue in his people in this life. Paul prays three times, that is, often, to be delivered from the thorn in the flesh, and all he can get is, “My grace is sufficient for you.” “I will graciously pardon you what I will not conquer in you,”#footnote[Though the weak Christian cannot open, read, and apply them, Christ can and will apply them to their souls. Jer 33:8. Isa 43:25. מחה, a Hebrew participle, denotes a constant, a continued act of God: I, I am he, blotting out your transgressions, today, and tomorrow, etc.] says God. “And I will cleanse them from all the iniquity they have committed against Me, and will forgive all their sins of rebellion against Me.” “I, yes I, am He who blots out your transgressions for My own sake and remembers your sins no more.” Ah, you#super[+] lamenting souls, who spend your days sighing and groaning under the sense and burden of your sins, why do you deal so unkindly with God, and so injuriously with your own souls, as not to cast an eye on those precious promises of the remission of sin, which may hold up and refresh your spirits in the darkest night, and under the heaviest burden of sin?

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to look on all your sins as charged to the account of Christ, as debts that the Lord Jesus has fully satisfied. And indeed, if there were but one farthing of the debt unpaid that Christ was bound to satisfy, it would not have agreed with the unspotted justice of God to let him come into heaven and sit down at his own right hand. But since all our debts have been discharged by his death, we are freed, and he is exalted to sit down at the right hand of his Father, which is the height of his glory, and the greatest pledge of our happiness. “God made Him who knew no sin to be sin on our#footnote[2 Cor 5:21. Christ was (#emph[peccatorum maximus]) the greatest of sinners by imputation and reputation. Isa 53:5, 6.] behalf, so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God,” says the apostle. All our sins were made to meet on Christ, as that evangelical prophet has it: “He was pierced for our transgressions, He was crushed for our iniquities; the punishment that brought us peace was upon Him, and by His stripes we are healed. We all like sheep have gone astray, each one has turned to his own way; and the #smallcaps[Lord] has laid upon Him the iniquity of us all”; or, as the Hebrew has it, he has made the iniquity of us all to meet in him. In law, we know, all the debts of the wife are charged to the husband. The wife says to one and to another, “If#footnote[Christ has the greatest worth and wealth in him. As the worth and value of many pieces of silver is in one piece of gold, so all the excellencies scattered abroad in the creatures are united in Christ.] I owe you anything, go to my husband.” So a believer may say to the law, and to the justice of God, “If I owe you anything, go to my Christ, who has undertaken for me.” I must not sit down discouraged at the thought of those debts that Christ has fully satisfied to the last farthing. Would it not show much weakness (I had almost said much madness) for a debtor to sit down discouraged when he looks over the debts that his surety has readily, freely, and fully satisfied? The sense of his surety’s great love should bind a man for#footnote[The whole volume of perfections that is spread through heaven and earth is summed up in him.] ever to love and honor his surety, and to bless the hand that has paid the debt and crossed out the account, etc. But to sit down discouraged when the debt is satisfied is a sin that calls for repentance, etc.

Christ has cleared all reckonings between God and us. You remember the scapegoat: “all the iniquities#footnote[Lev 16:21.] of the Israelites and all their rebellious acts in regard to all their sins” were confessed#footnote[Christ is (#emph[Canalis gratiae]) the channel of grace from God.] over its head and put on it, “and the goat will carry on itself all their iniquities,” etc. Why, the Lord Jesus is that blessed scapegoat on whom all our sins were laid, and who alone has carried #emph[our sins away into the land of forgetfulness, where they will never, never be remembered again.] A believer under the guilt of his sin may look the Lord in the face, and sweetly plead with him this way: “It is true, Lord, I owed you#footnote[The bloods of Abel, for so the Hebrew has it (as if the blood of one Abel had as many tongues as drops), cried for vengeance against sin; but the blood of Christ cries louder for the pardon of sin.] much, but your Son was my ransom, my redemption. His blood was the price; he was my surety, and undertook to answer for my sins. I know you must be satisfied, and Christ has satisfied you to the last farthing, not for himself, for what sins did he have of his own? But for me: they were my debts that he satisfied. Be pleased to look over the book, and you will find that it is crossed out by your own hand, on this very ground, that Christ has suffered and satisfied for them.”

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider the reasons why the Lord is pleased to have his people exercised, troubled, and vexed with the operations of sinful corruptions;#footnote[Augustine says that the first, second, and third virtue of a Christian is humility.] and they are these. Partly to keep them humble and low in their own eyes; and partly to set them on the use of all divine helps by which sin may be subdued and mortified; and partly that they may live on Christ for the perfecting of the work of sanctification;#footnote[#emph[Lilmod lelammed,] we learn so that we may teach, is a proverb among the rabbis. After the Trojans had been wandering and tossing up and down the Mediterranean Sea, as soon as they caught sight of Italy, they cried out with exulting joy, “Italy, Italy!” So will saints when they come to heaven.] and partly to wean them from things below, and to make them heartsick at their absence from Christ, and to keep alive in them a tender compassion toward others who are subject to the same weaknesses as they are; and that they may distinguish between a state of grace and a state of glory, and that heaven may be the sweeter to them in the end. Now since the Lord, for these weighty reasons, allows his people to be exercised and molested with the operations of sinful corruptions, oh, then let no believer speak, write, or conclude bitter things against his own soul and its comforts because sin troubles and vexes his righteous soul, etc. Let him rather lay his hand on his mouth and be silent, because the Lord will have it so, on such weighty grounds as the soul is not able to withstand.

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that believers must repent of their being#footnote[God never gave a believer a new heart so that it would always lie bleeding, and always be rent and torn in pieces with discouragements.] discouraged by their sins. Their being discouraged by their sins will cost them many a prayer, many a tear, and many a groan, because their discouragements under sin flow from ignorance and unbelief. They spring from their ignorance of the riches, freeness, fullness, and everlastingness of God’s love; and from their ignorance of the power, glory, sufficiency, and efficacy of the death and sufferings of the Lord Jesus Christ; and from their ignorance of the worth, glory, fullness, largeness, and completeness of the righteousness of Jesus Christ; and from their ignorance of the real, close, spiritual, glorious, and inseparable union that exists between Christ and their precious souls. Ah, if precious souls knew and believed the truth of these things as they should, they would not sit down dejected and overwhelmed under the sense and operations of sin, etc.
